# encoder_rotate_reverify_2026-08-03 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_rotate_reverify_2026-08-03.md and its data files.
#
# This measures NOTHING NEW about the encoder. It re-takes three already-published
# Darwin headlines under a protocol change, because all three were taken with a
# FIXED-ORDER interleave and a fixed-order interleave confounds ARM with POSITION
# (docs/DIFFERENTIAL_PLAYBOOK.md §6). No encoder source was modified in this
# session; every arm is a build of an already-landed commit.

date                 2026-08-03
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (Darwin arm64)
worktree             zenav1-aom--reverify2, detached (removed at the end of the session)
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f (v3.14.1),
                     prebuilt libaom.a copied in from the primary checkout (same submodule sha)
rustc                stable toolchain; NO -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch)
threading            single-threaded on every arm
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19` (eprof_ab.sh does it per invocation)
box load             NOT quiet. Two other agents were building and running test
                     suites on the same box throughout; `uptime` is stamped
                     before and after every band in the committed `<band>.env`
                     files, and one burst (a neighbour's `config_permutations`
                     test at ~960 % CPU) inflated encode times from ~150 ms to
                     ~250 ms inside the KB-PERF-4 rotated band. That is reported
                     rather than excluded — see the .md.

## The arms are REBUILT LANDED COMMITS, not patched trees

Each lever's `base` and `post` binaries are `benchmarks/xbench/drv-aom` built
`--release` from the two commits the original record names, in ONE worktree
checked out at each in turn (~/tmp/reverify2/build_arm.sh) and copied out:

  lever      base commit                                post commit
  KB-PERF-2  578653ff30b4116e29453b414c4da0702959ed63   99a10ab
  KB-PERF-3  590e525f89185a15b201a8a77316db7e7e6d3940   7976c0f
  KB-PERF-4  0279544522a77a7d6be81a66b02a53a1abdabed8   71c924a72a4221a42aee903386b6b5388a8c1fba

sha256 of the six executables (KB-PERF-3's post and KB-PERF-4's base are the
SAME BINARY to the byte — every commit between 7976c0f and 0279544 is docs,
bench or CI, so the compiler agreed; that is a free reproducibility check):

  p2base  83e48df3e085d5ca5aed5dadd47cc740137989dff1873ec46a79af1b2f1f0616
  p2post  5cc869bff1c34805f2474c7150dcf2c3a3dddd34182b9793dc45f9e37d44edc4
  p3base  4b768a228cc13823724eeebe04c4a1c16c4886aec419a65c4b0559e937e8a4f1
  p3post  3f2152558176490640e522f379963420d309093dd6a54fb601863e4e81e9585e
  p4base  3f2152558176490640e522f379963420d309093dd6a54fb601863e4e81e9585e
  p4post  b6cc2ab46087d4aefe2c76f7ca9f403b0ce9918fb737ecaa77d9b679df915023
  libaom-c e38ddada243b4e0b3aa568bab14358db6c1d2c44b934bfd454ca2f35e15739dd

Every null arm is a `cp` of its partner, so `base`/`baseB` are two FILES with
one sha, which is what "a same-binary null" has to mean.

## THE STALE-BINARY TRAP, and how it was avoided here

The build script leaves the worktree detached at whatever commit it built last,
so `scripts/eprof_ab.sh` read out of that worktree is NOT necessarily main's.
The first control band of this session was launched with the worktree still at
7976c0f and silently ran the PRE-ROTATE script: it completed, wrote a TSV with
no `position` column, and would have been read as a rotated band. It was caught
by the missing column and discarded, and the worktree was checked back out at
main (5d468f8) before any band was kept. Every kept band was run from a
worktree at 5d468f8 — `grep -c ROTATE scripts/eprof_ab.sh` = 2.

## Profile cell — unchanged from all five prior encoder studies

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv. Every arm in
every band emits the same 4472-byte stream, and all seven executables were
verified to produce the same `.obu` BY SHA256 before the first band ran.

## Exact commands

  git worktree add --detach ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--reverify2 main
  git -C ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--reverify2 submodule update --init
  cp -R ../zenav1-aom/upstream/build upstream/build      # same submodule sha

  # one drv-aom per named commit, copied out to ~/tmp/reverify2/bin/
  ~/tmp/reverify2/build_arm.sh <commit> <armname>
  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o ~/tmp/reverify2/bin/libaom-c \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c -Iupstream -Iupstream/build \
      -Lupstream/build -laom -lm -lc++

  # every band (ROT=1 rotated, ROT=0 fixed order); ~/tmp/reverify2/run_band.sh
  # stamps `uptime` into <out>.env before and after
  ROTATE=$ROT nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh $N <out.tsv> <arm=path>...
  python3 scripts/eprof_ab_position.py <out.tsv> --ref libaom-c --vs POST:PRE ...

## Bands

  control_rot.tsv     / control_fixed.tsv     6 copies of ONE binary,  36 rounds each
  control_rot120.tsv  / control_fixed120.tsv  6 copies of ONE binary, 120 rounds each
  p4_rot.tsv / p4_fixed.tsv   base/baseB/all/allB/libaom-c,     50 rounds each
  p3_rot.tsv / p3_fixed.tsv   base/baseB/both/bothB/libaom-c,   50 rounds each
  p2_rot.tsv / p2_fixed.tsv   base/baseB/final/finalB/libaom-c, 50 rounds each

Originals for comparison: KB-PERF-2 n=12 (5 arms, no null), KB-PERF-3 n=24
(7 arms, nulls both sides), KB-PERF-4 n=36 (6 arms, nulls both sides) — all
fixed order. Every band here MATCHES OR EXCEEDS its original's rounds.

The all-null control bands carry SIX copies of one binary rather than two, so
the position effect is estimated over the whole round rather than at one pair of
positions.

## The reading rule was pre-registered

~/tmp/reverify2/PREREGISTERED.md, written while the first lever band was at
round 17 of 50 and before any lever result was read; reproduced verbatim in the
.md §0. It fixes the resolution requirement (paired-mean MDE95 <= half the
published headline) and the escalation (more ROUNDS only — never a different
content, cell, arm set or statistic).

## What is NOT measured here

* Only Darwin. The Windows re-measurements of these levers
  (winperf_windows_2026-08-02.md, winperf_content_census_2026-08-03.md) were
  taken with their own harness and their own pooled-copy position correction and
  are NOT re-run here; nothing in this file changes them.
* Only the composed post-vs-base delta per lever. The per-half arms of the
  originals (`l3a`/`l3`, `col`/`row`, `z1z3`/`z2`) are not re-taken — a rotated
  re-measurement of a half needs its own band and its own nulls, and the halves
  were never the published headline.
* No new profile, no census, no instruction counts. This session changed no
  encoder source; `git diff` against main over crates/ is empty.
